      "meaning": "Look carefully at the beginning of Navarātri. We begin the worship of the family deity after completing the worship of the ancestors. Consider how Nāndi is called at weddings and upanayana ceremonies: after puṇyāha-vācana and worship of the feet, Nāndi is called; after calling Nāndi, the family deity is seated. That is how the sequence has been arranged. So, after receiving the blessings of the elders fully, Navarātri is celebrated to receive the blessings of the family deity. Navarātri is not merely worship of Durgā, Lakṣmī and Sarasvatī. Whatever our family deity is—Veṅkaṭaramaṇa, Narasiṃha or another deity—each family has its own deity. Worship that deity throughout these nine days and obtain the family deity’s grace. Mahālaya Pakṣa concerns the past: it is spoken of as setting our past right. Remember our elders. Who gave us this body, our intellectual capacities, our wonderful appearance and beauty? Our elders. We have thanked them. We remove the fear or anxiety in the mind [GPT reads añjike; native caption hanjike]; this is called gratitude therapy. Mahālaya concerns our DNA, our DNA—he speaks of “epigenetics” [captions say epik jenetiks, repeated]. Expressing gratitude and thanks to them, we begin Navarātri in the month of Āśvayuja.",
