          "english": "The devatās should come, and all the rākṣasas should go. Who are these rākṣasas? It is not as if some ghosts come and sit in the house. They are the bad qualities within us. Words spoken in the house remain there; their vibration remains there. If people quarrel daily, the vibration of quarrelling stays there. On entering the house, one feels, “Why did I come home?” Or we are [caption haluvutā, apparently speaking of weeping, is uncertain]. Not every day is happiness: one day happiness, one day sorrow, one day weeping [uncertain caption], one day telling our troubles, one day quarrelling or scolding [uncertain wording]. Such an atmosphere is present in the home, is it not? That bad atmosphere itself is the rākṣasas. Let all of them go out and let the divine element give us a sāttvika mind always: that is the intention with which Navarātri is celebrated, he says.",
